= BITwiki =

'''BITwiki''' is a semantic knowledge project built on [[MediaWiki]]. It combines authored pages and Book Matter, revision history, semantic properties and relationships, structured operational records, transclusion, queries, and programmable views into a durable knowledge substrate for humans and agents.

== Substrate ==

BITwiki's current technical stack separates responsibilities:

* MediaWiki provides pages, revisions, parsing, templates, transclusion, namespaces, permissions, and APIs;
* [[Semantic MediaWiki]] provides the durable semantic property/relationship graph;
* [[Cargo (MediaWiki extension)|Cargo]] provides repeated structured operational records where table-shaped state is appropriate;
* [[Scribunto]]/[[Lua]] provides deterministic normalization, type checking, bounded inference, transformation, and semantic presentation;
* [[BIThub]], built on [[Discourse]], provides the conversational and workflow interaction plane around the durable wiki.

The goal is not to collapse these systems into one mechanism. Each layer keeps a clear authority boundary while sharing canonical state through explicit interfaces.

== Programmable knowledge ==

A BITwiki page can combine canonical authored matter with structured identity, graph relationships, provenance/epistemic state, dynamic queries, and Lua-produced projections.

This allows pages to function as coherent reading surfaces while also supporting relationship panels, contextual navigation, comparisons, graph views, diagnostics, contribution tasks, and machine-facing representations without copying the underlying knowledge into separate systems.

See [[BITwiki:Programmable knowledge substrate]] and [[BITwiki:Lua architecture]].

== Relationship to BIThub ==

BIThub is where active discussion, research, coordination, and agent workflows can occur. BITwiki is where stable knowledge can become revisioned, linked, semantically typed, and reusable.

The systems connect through explicit APIs/plugins/services/workflows rather than by making Scribunto responsible for cross-service networking. BIThub should consume canonical BITwiki state; it should not need an independent competing ontology merely to provide a richer user interface.

